<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Modeling with Differential Equations</title>

  <exercise workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose a cylindrical tank with a hole in
        the bottom is filled with water.  The water
        will leak out and the height of the water in
        the tank will decrease.  Let's call the
        height <m>h(t)</m>.  Toricelli's Law says
        that
        <md>
          \frac{dh}{dt} = -k\sqrt{h}
        </md>
        where <m>k</m> is some constant.
      </p>
      <p>
        Suppose that the initial height of water in
        the tank is 100 cm and that, after five
        minutes, the height of water is 81 cm.
      </p>
      <p>
        Find the height in the tank as a function of
        time and sketch a graph of <m>h(t)</m>.  How
        long does it take for the water to drain out?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="33">
      <p>
        Separating variables gives us
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int h^{-1/2}~dh \amp = \int -k~dt
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            2h^{1/2} \amp = -kt + C
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            2\sqrt{h} \amp = -kt + C
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        Using <m>h(0) = 100</m>, we see that <m>C = 20</m>.
        Using <m>h(5) = 81</m>, we have
        <m>18 = 20 - 5k</m> so
        <m>k = \frac{2}{5}</m>.  Therefore,
        <md>
          h(t) = \left(10 - \frac{t}{5}\right)^2.
        </md>
        Setting <m>h = 0</m>, we see that the tank drains after
        <m>t = 50</m> minutes.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose that you continually put money into
        an account that earns interest at an annual
        rate of 6%, at the rate of $5,000 per year.
        Let <m>B(t)</m> be the balance of the
        account.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write a differential equation that
          expresses the rate of change <m>dB/dt</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="33">
        <p>
          <m>\frac{dB}{dt} = 0.06B + 5000</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve your differential equation for
          <m>B(t)</m> assuming that <m>B(0) = 0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="33">
        <p>
          This is a separable equation so
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \frac{dB}{dt} \amp = 0.06(B + \frac{250000}{3})
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \int \frac{1}{B+250000/3}~dB \amp = \int 0.06~dt
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \ln|B+250000/3| \amp = 0.06t+C
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              B \amp = Ae^{0.06t} - \frac{250000}{3}
            </mrow>
          </md>.
          Since <m>B(0) = 0</m>, we have <m>A = \frac{250000}{3}</m>,
          so
          <md>
            B(t) = \frac{250000}{3}
            \!\left(e^{0.06t} - 1\right).
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How long does it take to reach $50,000?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="33">
        <p>
          Setting <m>B(t) = 50000</m>, we have
          <m>e^{0.06t} = \frac{8}{5}</m>, so
          <md>
            t = \frac{\ln(8/5)}{0.06} \approx 7.8
          </md>
          years.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose that you take out a mortgage of
        $100,000 at an interest rate of 4%.  Let
        <m>M(t)</m> be the amount that you owe on
        the mortgage after <m>t</m> years, so
        <m>M(0) = 100{,}000</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The interest you are paying causes the
          amount you owe to increase.  Suppose that
          you are also paying <m>r</m> dollars per
          year on the mortgage.  Set up a
          differential equation that describes
          <m>dM/dt</m>, the rate at which the amount
          you owe is changing.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="33">
        <p>
          <m>\frac{dM}{dt} = 0.04M - r</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the differential equation in terms
          of <m>r</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="33">
        <p>
          Separating variables gives
          <m>M = 25r + Ae^{0.04t}</m>.  Using
          <m>M(0) = 100000</m>, we have
          <md>
            M(t) = 25r + (100000 - 25r)e^{0.04t}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If you want to pay off the mortgage after
          30 years, what should the value of
          <m>r</m> be?  What is your monthly
          mortgage payment?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="33">
        <p>
          Setting <m>M(30) = 0</m>, we have
          <md>
            r = \frac{4000e^{1.2}}{e^{1.2}-1}
            \approx \$5{,}724
          </md>
          per year, or about <m>\$477</m> per month.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How much will you have paid over the
          30 years?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="33">
        <p>
          <m>30 \times 5724 \approx
          \$171{,}700</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the monthly mortgage payment if
          you want to pay off the mortgage after
          20 years?  How much will you have paid
          over the 20 years?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="33">
        <p>
          Setting <m>M(20) = 0</m>, we have
          <md>
            r = \frac{4000e^{0.8}}{e^{0.8}-1}
            \approx \$7{,}264
          </md>
          per year, or about <m>\$605</m> per month.
          The total paid is
          <m>20 \times 7264 \approx
          \$145{,}300</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
